{
  "title": "Pencil lead and diamond are both carbon. So why so different? | So That's Why",
  "description": "Little Q drew a whole picture, but one swipe of her hand smudged it, and her palm turned black too. The Chief says that black layer on her hand and a sparkly diamond are both made of carbon. Same carbon, so how can one rub right off while the other is the hardest to scratch?\n\nIn this episode, the Chief and Little Q follow one pencil all the way: first they take pencil lead apart to see what's really mixed inside; then they build carbon out of a box of building blocks and let it make guesses: which one slides, which one is harder, and whether the hardest means unbreakable. Finally, a pencil line actually lights up a tiny bulb, but a diamond can't. Why?\n\nThe illustrations in this episode were made with AI; the blocks are a model, and real carbon isn't wooden balls. The numbers are approximate. For the pencil-line experiment, use only a small battery, and never plug anything into a wall socket at home. The source of every claim is listed in the references below.\n\nInformation as of 2026-11-09",
  "tags": [
    "pencil",
    "diamond",
    "graphite",
    "carbon",
    "hardness",
    "electricity",
    "science for kids",
    "So That's Why"
  ],
